WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.630
It seems like everything
is going green these days

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.390
but there's one mode of transportation

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:08.520
that seems to be clinging

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.863
to the traditional, fuel-loving ways.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.890
And while commercial
aviation has made strides

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.520
through smarter design
and cleaner engines,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.295
many of those gains have been nullified by

00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:28.060
more air traffic.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.570
The challenge is enormous.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.070
This is a large industry.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.203
It will take decades to convert.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.950
The good news is that we
will have a solution as early

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.340
as three years out that
people can get into

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.413
and start moving zero emission.

00:00:44.332 --> 00:00:45.881
And it turns out that the solution

00:00:45.881 --> 00:00:48.200
could be all around us.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.940
Hydrogen is the most abundant
element in the universe.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.940
And so if you had to use
something, it would be great.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.030
The only product of the chemical reaction

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.843
between hydrogen and oxygen is water.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.029
We looked at the fundamentals

00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:18.960
of what it would require to
take an aircraft up in the air

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.180
of significant size, over
significant distance,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.420
commercially relevant.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.330
You get your hydrogen fuel
cell being the best approach

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.846
from the cost of fuel, efficiency of

00:01:27.846 --> 00:01:29.507
utilization of the fuel

00:01:29.507 --> 00:01:33.465
and the mitigation of the climate effects.

00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:36.440
Serial cleantech
entrepreneur Val Miftakhov

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.490
started ZeroAvia following
his previous success

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.193
in the EV charging industry.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.710
So the beauty about hydrogen in general is

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.930
that the energy density
of hydrogen as fuel

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.599
is actually three times
better than jet fuel.

00:01:51.599 --> 00:01:55.049
So you can see any size of

00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:57.432
aircraft going for any distance

00:01:57.432 --> 00:02:00.650
that jet fuel aircraft can go over time.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.270
It will just take significant amount

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.230
of time to get the industry over,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:08.170
but this technology can scale to all sizes

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.470
of aircraft that we use
in commercial service.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.920
ZeroAvia uses a hydrogen fuel cell

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.223
to produce electricity
to turn a propeller.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.640
Unlike a traditional engine
which uses combustion

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.910
to create energy, a fuel
cell generates electricity

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.160
through an electrochemical reaction.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.860
In this case, hydrogen
and oxygen are combined

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.223
to generate electricity, heat and water.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.180
ZeroAvia has flown a six-seater aircraft

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.163
on a hydrogen fuel cell; a world first.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.000
Now they're aiming bigger, at 20 seats.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.908
That's technically commercial.

00:02:45.908 --> 00:02:48.880
One side of the aircraft,
the left side, will replace

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.240
the engine with our power plants.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.250
We still got a normal
engine on the right side.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.116
And part of this is, in
aviation you want to ramp up

00:02:58.116 --> 00:03:00.820
risk profile in meaningful steps.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.940
So anything happens, we
have the second engine.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.220
But even in the first
flight test campaign,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.130
what we're planning to do
is to demonstrate operation

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.060
of this aircraft purely
on zero emission power

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.180
on the left side engine.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.360
Once we take off, we're
able to switch over

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.150
to completely zero emission power.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.940
Hydrogen is used throughout
all sections of a flight,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.430
that maximizes the efficiency
of the entire operation

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.060
that reduces the weight,
provides for best sort

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.823
of mission capabilities,
payload and range.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.420
Building power plants and
fuel cells is one thing,

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.120
creating a whole new infrastructure

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.353
for supplying hydrogen is
something entirely different.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.320
In automotive a big part of the reason

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.310
why hydrogen did not take off is

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.870
because the fueling infrastructure needs

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.553
to be so distributed.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.110
For let's say, United States,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.630
you have a hundred
thousand fueling stations.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.060
Compare that with aviation
where 95% or more traffic

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.580
in the United States is
concentrated in 100 locations.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.100
So that's a three order
magnitude difference

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.120
which makes build out of the
infrastructure much simpler.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.890
It's much larger, much
more concentrated stations,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:21.293
but they're much fewer in quantity.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.570
Calling it simpler might be
underselling the challenge

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.480
of distributing hydrogen,
which unlike other fuels

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.210
that can be easily
transported in liquid form,

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.023
is usually found instead
in a gaseous state.

00:04:35.023 --> 00:04:37.670
How do you get the hydrogen
from point A to point B?

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.610
We don't have pipelines
from moving hydrogen around.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.360
We don't have all the specialized trucks

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.200
to move them around

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.290
and so we got thinking, we need a solution

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:50.010
that can be a low capital
expenditure solution

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.613
and that's the genesis of the company.

00:04:52.613 --> 00:04:54.990
John-Paul Clarke is the co-founder

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.130
and Chief Innovation Officer
of Universal Hydrogen,

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.070
a startup that has designed
a modular tank system

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.030
for the domestic turbo prop market.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.240
Like ZeroAvia, it allows
for retrofitting planes

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.910
already in use.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.200
Each module has two capsules

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.450
and so what we do is
load it into the aircraft

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:17.170
as if it was cargo, strap it down,

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.300
connect it to the aircraft,
close the loading door,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.430
and that would be it.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:26.260
When you get to your destination,
you'd basically unload it,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.258
put it back in a truck, send
it back to the production site

00:05:31.258 --> 00:05:32.820
to get refills.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.160
We needed to basically
come up with something

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.080
that could both fit in containers

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.930
and also fit in the aircraft,
not require increases

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.230
in the maximum takeoff
weight of the aircraft.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.510
This compromise is one
of the biggest hurdles

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:50.720
that may prevent widespread
adoption of hydrogen.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.750
Obviously, you're gonna
have to take out some seats

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.150
because the energy density
of hydrogen is less

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.140
than jet fuel and you can't store it

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.610
in the wings practically,
so you're gonna have

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.610
to take away some space in the fuselage.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.570
In the ultra-fine margins of aviation,

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:12.660
removing 10 to 20% of
your seats is a tough ask,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:13.880
but that hasn't deterred startups

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.043
like ZeroAvia or Universal Hydrogen.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:22.150
The maintenance cost of a fuel
cell motor system goes down

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.050
and it goes down
significantly, because motors

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.690
and fuel cells have
much fewer moving parts

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.270
than a gas turbine engine.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:36.070
And so the wear and tear is much lower

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.780
and therefore the time
between overhaul is longer.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:44.260
And so when you put all that
together, our numbers indicate

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.710
that the CASM, Cost per
Available Seat Mile,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.510
actually goes down slightly
or is at the worst equivalent

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:52.343
to what you have now.

00:06:52.343 --> 00:06:56.080
So what you'll have is a smaller cabin

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.390
or smaller number of seats.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:01.130
However, the cost for each of those seats

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:05.223
to operate it is the same
or better with the hydrogen.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.200
Like ZeroAvia,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.140
Universal Hydrogen is
also working on engines,

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.950
successfully testing their
two megawatt iron bird

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:17.110
that will allow them to
retrofit planes carrying up

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.810
to 55 passengers.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:20.860
ZeroAvia is aiming

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.520
for their first commercial
hydrogen electric flight

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.190
between London and Rotterdam
with their 19-seater

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:28.680
by around 2024.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.890
But much like the range
anxiety that has plagued

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.680
some battery-powered
electric vehicles on land,

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.020
hydrogen fuel cells are
also fairly limited.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.930
At this point, they still
don't have the capacity

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.233
to power a common 100-passenger jet.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.960
Beyond fuel cells, however,
there is another hope

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.730
for our lightest, most abundant element:

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:48.563
Burning it.

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.243
The first eureka moment,
I suppose, is when we saw

00:07:55.243 --> 00:07:56.343
steam in the exhaust

00:07:56.343 --> 00:07:58.280
because we are thinking
to ourselves, "Huh?

00:07:58.280 --> 00:07:59.400
We're burning something
here when you're producing

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.660
absolutely no CO2."

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.870
An expert in gas turbine combustion,

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.330
professor Bobby Sethi leads research

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:09.200
at Cranfield University in the U.K.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.050
Built on a former RAF base,

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:14.220
the college runs multiple
aeronautical programs.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.890
But what Bobby is focused
on is burning hydrogen,

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.040
as cleanly as possible.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:20.280
So what we are trying to demonstrate

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.900
in this rig is how we can conceive

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.040
some hydrogen combustion
technologies that can be integrated

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.240
in the next generation aircraft engines,

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.440
which will deliver not
only zero CO2 emissions,

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.283
but also ultra-low NOx emissions.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:36.900
NOx or nitrogen oxides
are a significant source

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.000
of air pollution globally.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.920
They're the dirty particulates
that cause smog in cities,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:45.293
usually spat out by diesel
cars, scooters and buses.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:47.880
Hydrogen is characterized

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.000
by much wider flammability limits,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.870
which means we can go to
much leaner combustion.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.290
And as a result, we can burn

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:57.110
at much lower flame
temperatures and that's better

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:58.653
for reducing NOx emissions.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.760
Going leaner means burning
fuel with an excess

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.000
of air in the engine.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.750
Using something like
gasoline, lean burn emits

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.840
far fewer hydrocarbons.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:11.853
Doing it with hydrogen also
delivers cleaner emissions.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:21.920
While NOx is a pollutant,
hydrogen combustion produces up

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.140
to 90% less nitrogen oxide than kerosene.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.700
So while burning hydrogen
isn't technically as clean

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.750
as using it inside a fuel cell,

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:31.950
it's still a huge
improvement over jet fuel.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.700
Unfortunately, like
any radically new idea,

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.220
there are other potential
byproducts of the process

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.003
that aren't fully yet understood.

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.690
NOx is clearly one of the
main emissions we need

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:46.060
to consider, but the aviation community

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.380
is also asking themselves
and the community in general,

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:51.230
what about all the water vapor emissions?

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.300
And some studies have shown,

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.350
albeit there's still a large
degree of uncertainty about it,

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.550
that contrails and cirrus clouds

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:01.250
that may be induced from
contrails could contribute

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:05.240
to global warming about four
times the amount than CO2 does.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.550
We know that if we are going
to be burning hydrogen,

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:09.410
we are going to produce much larger amount

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.100
of water vapor emissions.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.280
And if we are going to be
producing a much larger amount

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.590
of water vapor emissions,
then the propensity

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:18.040
for contrail formation is
also going to increase.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.620
In spite of all these challenges,

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.200
hydrogen is being taken seriously

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.050
by the broader industry.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:26.517
Companies like Universal Hydrogen

00:10:26.517 --> 00:10:29.880
and ZeroAvia are gaining the
attention of investors looking

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.783
for viable and eventually
profitable solutions.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:36.200
Industry giant Airbus wants

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.880
to introduce a hydrogen-powered
passenger aircraft by 2035.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:42.050
Recently announcing plans

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:44.910
to retrofit a gas-guzzling Superjumbo

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.603
with a hydrogen-burning engine.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.870
The modified aircraft
will add a fifth engine,

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.120
adapted for hydrogen, and will be mounted

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.293
on the rear fuselage.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:57.920
The A380 is the largest
passenger aircraft in existence,

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.810
so it offers plenty of room

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.163
to store the 400 kilograms of hydrogen.

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.640
The company's designs for
a blended wing concept

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.490
to store extra hydrogen
also offer a glimpse

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:09.683
of our flying future.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.340
Apart from the complex
engineering challenges faced

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.420
by airplane manufacturers,
there are also difficulties

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:18.670
in production of hydrogen itself.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:21.940
Nowadays, most of the hydrogen
used in fuel is derived

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:25.530
by splitting it off from
molecules of natural gas.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.330
But that requires a good deal of energy

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.223
and also produces carbon dioxide.

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.890
To make green hydrogen,
the electricity used

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:34.420
to run the electrolyzer must come

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.260
from a renewable resource,

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.040
which is currently a lot more expensive.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.170
As with any new technology however,

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:42.720
initial costs are daunting,

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.603
but time may be the best remedy here.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:50.350
If you look at economics of
hydrogen fuel production,

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.410
for example, versus fossil
fuels, for instance,

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:55.500
the cost of hydrogen is all based

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.790
on capital expense and very
little operating expense.

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.960
So it's sort of similar to solar power.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:02.950
You put solar panels out there,

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.730
and they produce power for 20-25 years.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.050
The operating expense is relatively low.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.660
What that means is that as scale grows,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.270
as we've seen with the solar panels,

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:16.363
the cost of output drops dramatically.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.040
So the first phase is not about

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.350
the most efficient aircraft.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:22.470
It's not about the aircraft

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.890
that will deliver the
lowest NOx emissions.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:26.110
It's about demonstrating

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:28.300
that we can carry
hydrogen safely on board,

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.880
we can burn it safely on board,

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.070
and can be used to fly
a passenger aircraft.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:35.100
The key thing is that we
don't try to put everything

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:36.080
in the first generation

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.290
or we're just gonna delay
the entry into service.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.490
So to try and keep it
as simple as possible.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.790
If there are some questions on the margin,

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:50.150
they're all in the sort of business model

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:52.870
and market adoption realm.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:57.386
It is very hard to create a

00:12:57.386 --> 00:12:58.894
technological argument

00:12:58.894 --> 00:13:01.320
or impossible to create
a technological argument

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.180
that says, "Well, it's not gonna work."

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:05.350
It is going to work.

